#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180508} :activity/id #uuid "564a18a1-f71e-5757-9e58-d7176c63906f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049617 :topic/id #uuid "04ff54ad-5d9d-5751-8a07-6602f0f05410" :topic/math-academy-id 3717} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180726 :tutorial/content "Let $\\psi(x,y)$ be a two-variable function defined on a simply connected open set $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2.$ We know that for a point $(x_0,y_0)\\in D,$ the vector\n\n$$\n\\left.\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf i -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf j\\right)\\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}\n$$\n\nis *tangential* to the level curve $\\psi(x,y) = \\psi(x_0,y_0).$ \n\nBy computing this tangent vector at *every* point, we can generate a vector *field* tangential to the level curves of $\\psi$ at every point.\n\nWith that in mind, let \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)= P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n \n\nbe a vector field defined on $D.$ A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is said to be a **stream function** of $\\mathbf F$ if it satisfies\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \nQ = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's consider the function \n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\nTo construct a vector field with this stream function, we first compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the vector field with the stream function $\\psi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j=2y\\,i-2x\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA diagram showing the surface $z = \\psi(x,y),$ its level curves, and the vector field $\\mathbf F$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/69/696e6c623dbc3ecfae2643a7317d3d176294624289ecf6efb16debdc947d0394.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The level curves of $\\psi(x,y)$ are circles of the form $x^2+y^2=c$, and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ in the $xy$-plane is tangential to these level curves.\n\n- Suppose $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity of a fluid in the $xy$-plane. Then, the level curves of $\\psi(x,y)$ represent the path of a massless particle in the $xy$-plane moving with the flow. For this reason, the level curves of $\\psi$ are called **streamlines.**\n\n- The stream function of a vector field is typically not unique since we can add any constant to $\\psi$ to generate another valid stream function." :tutorial/id #uuid "9feba9cd-6bee-5c74-a1ab-940f686f38e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5856 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8704325f-a4d0-5908-8630-87a2fdd6ce55" :step/math-academy-id 35867 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180551}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180551 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179765 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179426 :question/id #uuid "a779f67a-21ee-5eb4-8355-c69476ee0d3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12395"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7abceeb-620d-5e95-93ca-30bc557cf007" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050412}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490304}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Vector Field From Its Stream Function"} :step/id #uuid "f95a8abc-f190-54e3-ab9c-c5ca8d31c613" :step/math-academy-id 44491 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179766 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179427 :question/id #uuid "69a293cc-ff8c-5485-8329-12e87e1addff" :question/math-academy-id "e-12396"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc613be0-2d34-5253-8fbe-7d2a5fcd0840" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050189}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490318}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Cases Where a Stream Function Exists"} :step/id #uuid "0aa49923-0dc0-553a-a533-d846d4f56429" :step/math-academy-id 44492 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180566}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179774 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179435 :question/id #uuid "6cb53e89-c4a9-57a6-b54f-f6a09d3a17d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12439"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f30cae81-5bda-52dc-8de4-9ad5fd587e48" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490212}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Streamlines of Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "dcb816eb-91e7-58ce-82b1-214fe5d095a2" :step/math-academy-id 44698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180566 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180759 :tutorial/content "Let's find a stream function for the following vector field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^x \\sin y\\,\\mathbf{i} + e^x \\cos y\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nNote that our vector field $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ has the simply connected domain $D=\\mathbb{R}^2,$ where \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^x\\sin y, \\qquad\nQ(x,y) = e^x\\cos y.\n$$\n\nLet's start by checking that a stream function exists. Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf F$ has a stream function since $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ if \n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y} = e^x\\sin y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x} = -e^x\\cos y.\n$$\n\nTo find a stream function, we **partially integrate** these two equations and compare the results:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y} = e^x\\sin y\n$$\n\nPartially integrating this equation with respect to $y$ (that is, treating $x$ as though it were a constant), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y+f(x) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+f(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is an arbitrary function.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x} = -e^x\\cos y.\n$$\n\nPartially integrating this equation with respect to $x$ (that is, treating $y$ as though it were a constant), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =\\int -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+g(y) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is an arbitrary function.\n\nComparing our two expressions for $\\psi,$ we get equality by setting $f=g=0.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = -e^x \\cos y.\n$$\n\nFinally, remember that the streamlines of $\\mathbf F$ are given by the level curves of $\\psi.$ So, to find the streamlines of $\\mathbf F,$ we plot the curves $\\psi = k$ for different values of $k.$ The level curves of $\\psi$ are always tangential to $\\mathbf F.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bc3ea425-1287-5a1d-ad21-3528a9c3d57e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7704 :tutorial/title "Calculating a Stream Function From Its Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "173559da-bde2-5974-ae89-c0d7f7949615" :step/math-academy-id 44700 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180571}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179437 :question/id #uuid "6cf11fc9-874f-5d59-bff0-1ed3debcbd40" :question/math-academy-id "e-12446"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffa79034-bdcc-5524-aa39-e81a79b48ded" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401357}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Stream Function for a Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "f7a796a3-baa3-5a37-a25b-6048cc6cce34" :step/math-academy-id 44750 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180565}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180763 :tutorial/content "Not all vector fields have an associated stream function! So, it's useful to have some way of determining whether a given vector field has a stream function or not. For this, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Let $\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field defined in a simply connected open set $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2.$ The vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ if and only if*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ must be zero everywhere for a stream function to exist. \n\nWe won't give a detailed proof of this result here, but it's quite easy to see why it may be true. \n\nRecall that if $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ if it satisfies\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \nQ = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the divergence of this vector field, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y}-\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂y∂x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y}-\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "61b21c71-3509-5455-86ba-1898b599345d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7744 :tutorial/title "An Existence Condition for Stream Functions"} :step/id #uuid "fb48f733-4224-542a-8186-b1bda7425749" :step/math-academy-id 44836 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180552}}] :activity/title "Stream Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179868}]}
